    {
      "question": "e-5616",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "66d991d16c397a8e9f9b6cb708b909cfedb842f513d496a703745a1ca1ca510e",
      "new_sha256": "186af6c8e48d47c0fd25fda7237382c38f94fe44ceae6637c6a4e419b0993ed2",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5616",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5616.json",
          "file_sha256": "3b4f2512628e39d087bdddefb3add655c8480af533628e8026e3dce63159be21"
