    {
      "question": "e-5441",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "c35204e72078525cdc4ff39db9d18ce9a614a9a95f1d4c2d93a0c5b63bf4f035",
      "new_sha256": "af5c0240fc1c54852fb944b936a25c1bda0db13123a07a199a064be7388f3b4a",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5441",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5441.json",
          "file_sha256": "05c69eb6d107d3e9b2b98d440c518af7e99288d2be7883f1dd0020debdc36d6e"
        }
